    \centering

    \caption{
    Upper left: Schematic of the interferometric phase-estimation setup. A single photon propagates through a balanced Mach--Zehnder interferometer containing an unknown phase shift $\phi$ and a controllable phase shift $\theta$, which is controlled by the CQL protocol. Upper right: Corresponding loss landscape, $\mathcal{L}(\theta,\phi)=\sin^2[(\theta-\phi)/2]$, whose global minimum identifies the unknown phase. Main: Time evolution of the probability distribution over the discretized parameter register, $|\Psi(\theta,t)|^2$. Starting from a uniform superposition, the coherent dynamics progressively localize the probability amplitude around the global minimum of the loss landscape, yielding an accurate estimate of the unknown phase $\phi$.
